[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115646,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "<p>Leiomyoma is a benign tumour with smooth muscle differentiation. Angioleiomyoma is a pericytic neoplasm comprising a proliferation of well-differentiated smooth muscle cells surrounding vascular channels.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-10T14:06:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115647,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8890/0 Leiomyoma, NOS</p>\r\n<p>8894/0 Angioleiomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115648,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E86.1 Leiomyoma of other or unspecified sites</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115649,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> myoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115650,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "<p>None</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-10T14:06:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115651,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common sites for leiomyoma in the head and neck region are the oral cavity (lips, tongue, buccal mucosa, palate), sinonasal tract, and mandible {24250094; 26094018; 26682845}. They are extraordinarily rare in the sinonasal tract, predominantly involving the nasal cavity and more rarely the paranasal sinuses. In the sinonasal tract they mostly present as angioleiomyoma {26047608; 12653572; 24384850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115652,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>At most sites the tumours cause soft tissue swellings, whereas mucosal lesions may cause nasal or airway obstruction, bleeding, and (rarely) pain {26047608; 12653572; 24384850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115653,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Only about 1% of all leiomyomas are located in the head and neck region {24250094}. Of these, about 12&ndash;15% present as angioleiomyomas {26047608; 1122481; 12653572; 24250094}. Adults are most often affected, with an equal sex distribution.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115654,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115655,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is no evidence that leiomyomas of the head and neck region share the driver mutations (<em>MED12</em>, <em>HMGA2</em> rearrangements) seen in genital and retroperitoneal leiomyomas {22223266; 28591699}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115656,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyomas are polypoid, nodular, and usually sharply demarcated, with a white to tan trabecular cut surface covered by a capsule or normal mucosa {30885006}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115657,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyomas are subepithelial with infrequent mucosal ulceration. Bland, spindled tumour cells are arranged in intersecting fascicles. Cells are oval to elongate, with cigar-shaped nuclei, without atypia, and with eosinophilic fibrillary cytoplasm. Mitoses are absent to sparse.</p>\r\n<p>Angioleiomyoma, the most common smooth muscle tumour in the head and neck region, shows a prominent vasculature surrounded by smooth muscle cells intimately associated with vessels {24384850}. Calcification, ossification, fatty metaplasia, or myxohyaline degeneration may be seen, probably indicating longstanding lesions. A fatty component is more common in male patients and older patients. Although it has a smooth muscle component, angioleiomyoma is formally classified as a pericytic (perivascular) tumour (see the <em>Soft tissue and bone tumours</em> volume).</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, lesions express smooth muscle markers (&alpha;-SMA, MSA, desmin, and caldesmon) but are negative for HMB45, SOX10, and S100 {26047608; 12653572; 24111893}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115658,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 213,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T11:05:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
